    "knowledge_point": "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine Under a Modified Domain",
    "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968930/assets/caa6662d8349751e35bcb1af4196ad043b66ea64f21805eb6ca9b14435d38458.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$",
